= Wave 6 compiled context =

'''State: Phase 2 — Context compilation + writing.'''

Wave 6 builds formal-computation and computer-systems foundations over prior [[Computation]], [[Algorithm]], [[Programming language]], [[Logic]], semantics, systems, and cognition.

== Dependency order ==

<pre>
Formal language
 └→ Automaton (computation)
      ├→ State machine
      └→ Turing machine
           ├→ Computability
           ├→ Turing completeness
           └→ Computational complexity

Computer architecture
 └→ Operating system
      ├→ Compiler
      ├→ Database
      └→ Computer network
             └→ Distributed system

Computer security
 └→ Cryptography

Human-computer interaction

Esoteric programming language
 └→ Programming language / computation boundary exploration
</pre>

== Normalized definitions ==

=== Formal language ===
A formal language is a mathematically specified set of finite strings over an alphabet.

=== Automaton (computation) ===
A computational automaton is an abstract machine defined by states, inputs and transition rules, often used to recognize or transform formal languages.

=== State machine ===
A state machine models behavior as states plus transitions triggered by inputs, events or conditions.

=== Turing machine ===
A Turing machine is an abstract model of computation using finite control, a read/write head, tape and transition rules.

=== Computability ===
Computability studies which functions and problems can be solved by effective procedures under formal models of computation.

=== Turing completeness ===
A computational system is Turing complete when it can simulate a universal Turing machine under idealized unbounded resources.

=== Computational complexity ===
Computational complexity studies the resources required to solve computational problems as functions of input size.

=== Computer architecture ===
Computer architecture concerns the organization and programmer-visible structure of computer systems, including instruction sets, processors, memory and I/O.

=== Operating system ===
An operating system manages hardware resources and provides abstractions and services for programs and users.

=== Computer network ===
A computer network is a set of interconnected computing devices that exchange data using communication links and protocols.

=== Database ===
A database is an organized collection of persistent data; a DBMS is software used to manage databases.

=== Compiler ===
A compiler transforms a source representation, usually a program, into a target representation while preserving intended semantics to the required degree.

=== Distributed system ===
A distributed system coordinates computation across multiple autonomous networked components.

=== Computer security ===
Computer security protects systems and information against unauthorized access, modification, disclosure, disruption and related failures.

=== Cryptography ===
Cryptography designs mathematical mechanisms and protocols for properties such as confidentiality, integrity, authentication and verifiability.

=== Human-computer interaction ===
Human-computer interaction studies the design, use and evaluation of interactive computational systems for human use.

=== Esoteric programming language ===
An esoteric programming language is designed primarily for conceptual, experimental, artistic, playful or boundary-testing purposes rather than ordinary production utility.

