== Theoretical-computation boundary ==

The writing spine is:

<pre>
formal language ↔ recognizer / generator
                 ↓
        automaton model
                 ↓
      Turing-computability
           ↙           ↘
  computability       complexity
</pre>

A problem can be computable but computationally intractable.

A language/system can be Turing complete while being unusable in practice.

The Church–Turing thesis links effective calculability to formal models; equivalence among formal models does not itself prove the thesis.

== Systems-stack boundary ==

<pre>
instruction-set / hardware organization
              ↓
     computer architecture
              ↓
        operating system
              ↓
 programs / compilers / DBMS / applications
              ↓
       networks / distributed systems
</pre>

The arrows are dependency/orientation aids, not strict layer exclusivity.

== Security boundary ==

Security depends on a threat model and system context.

Cryptography supplies mechanisms, but a cryptographically strong primitive can be deployed insecurely.

Core security concepts should preserve confidentiality, integrity, availability, authentication, authorization, accountability and resilience distinctions where relevant.

== HCI boundary ==

HCI spans usability, accessibility, interaction design, human factors, cognition, social context and evaluation.

It must not collapse into frontend aesthetics or graphical-interface layout.

== Esoteric-language boundary ==

Esolangs are legitimate programming-language objects.

They can test minimal instruction sets, unusual execution models, notation, computational universality, pedagogy and programming culture.

Their lack of ordinary production utility is often intentional and does not invalidate them as programming languages.

